GERI HALLIWELL TO VISIT NO LONGER WAR-TORN KOSOVO
"I want to see the children from my hotel," she declares.

Six weeks after the war ended, ex-Spice girl Geri Halliwell feels that
it is now safe to go to ravaged Yugoslavia to see what all the fuss is
about.

Ms. Halliwell, 45 although she maintains she's only 27, was appointed
peace commissioner by the United Nations in 1998, and has successfully
juggled this daunting position with a high profile and flourishing
career in the world of pop.  Her debut single, "Look At Me," only failed
to reach the coveted top spot in England's pop charts by not selling
enough records.  However, her dancing and vogueing will be of no use to
her when she jets off to the once battle-strewn Kosovo.

"As I sat at home watching those dreadful pictures of war, poverty,
sickness and disease on my television night after night, I had just one
thought," said Ms. Halliwell, tears welling.  "That was to have the war
finish as soon as possible so that I could take an all-expenses paid
trip out there, visit the city, tut loudly for a while, and look at the
children from my hotel room."

In a brave move, it's believed that Ms. Halliwell intends to actually
stay in the city itself.  The recently rebuilt Rektvar Hotel in downtown
Kosovo is the only building in the city with running water, and walls.
Staff manager Thverard Hrugvar said, "Yes, we are expecting Ms.
Halliwell soon.  Her people made arrangements last week, and we have
taken delivery of 4000 litres of Evian, 230 loaves of French bread, 2000
pounds of fresh meat, and several litres of paint with which we have
been instructed to 'brighten up her suite a bit'."

From the comfort of her 9th floor luxury room, the only one in the city
believed to have a carpet, Ms. Halliwell will stroll around making
discouraging noises with her mouth, and waiting for a big enough crowd
to gather outside.  At some point during the nine-day stopover which is
paid for by us taxpayers, Ms. Halliwell may step onto her balcony, and
wave briefly from behind a dark pair of sunglasses, before going back
inside.  If she doesn't feel like doing this, an aide will pretend to be
her.

Gamanak Fturak, who used to own a shop, greeted the news of Ms.
Halliwell's impending arrival by shouting "Ginger!  Ginger!  Ginger!"
loudly before being crushed to death by a NATO tank.

